Bidirectional Tristate Bus Port (Parameterized Width)

Figure: Bidirectional Tristate Bus Port (Parameterized Width)
Problem Statement

Bidirectional bus ports are the foundation of GPIO pads, memory data buses, and chip-level I/O interfaces. A bidirectional port allows a single physical wire to carry data in both directions, controlled by an output enable signal. When the output enable is active, the module drives data onto the bus; when inactive, the bus is released to high-impedance state so other drivers can take control.

Design a synthesizable combinational module bidir_bus with a parameterized width.

The module receives a parameterized data input data_in, an output enable signal oe, and exposes a bidirectional bus port bus. It also provides a data output data_out that reflects the current state of the bus. When oe is active, the module drives data_in onto the bus. When oe is inactive, the bus must be in high-impedance state. The data output always reflects the bus state regardless of the output enable. The bus width is controlled by a parameter WIDTH with a default value of 8.
Input/Output Examples:

Scenario A (WIDTH=8, single-bit example):
+---------+----+-----+----------+
| data_in | oe | bus | data_out |
+---------+----+-----+----------+
|    0    | 0  |  z  |    z     |
|    0    | 1  |  0  |    0     |
|    1    | 0  |  z  |    z     |
|    1    | 1  |  1  |    1     |
+---------+----+-----+----------+


Answer 

module biderctional #(paramatersized width = 8 )(
   input [width-1:0] data_in ,
   input oe,
   input [width-1:0] bus,
   outut [width-1:0] data_out
);

   assign bus = oe ? data_in : {width{1'bz}};
   assign data_out = bus;

endmodule 
